# No AI Slop

Prefer less. If it is not required for the current request, do not add it.

## Scope and structure

- Do only what was asked. No bonus features, folders, or abstractions “for later”.
- If the stack is unclear, ask before scaffolding. Do not invent a full stack.
- Start as a single app. No monorepo (`apps/`, `packages/`) until there is a real need.
- No empty files, barrel `index.ts` spam, placeholder tests, or dead `utils`/`helpers`/`common`.
- No unsolicited CI, Docker, Makefile, LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING, or long README.

## Dependencies

- Add a library only when there is a concrete use case in code.
- Prefer the stack’s built-ins over overlapping third-party packages.

## Naming and code

- Names must describe the real job. Avoid vague `Manager`, `Helper`, `Service`, `Util`.
- No comments or JSDoc that restate the obvious.
- No premature clean-architecture layers, custom exception trees, or wrappers for one call site.
- Prefer deleting unused code over leaving TODOs and stubs that look complete.

## Copy and tone

- No marketing filler: seamless, robust, leverage, unlock, cutting-edge, delight, etc.
- UI/docs copy stays short and specific. Not a landing-page pitch unless requested.

## Emoji

- Do not use emoji by default in UI, README, commits, comments, logs, or errors.
- Use emoji only when branding or a platform convention truly requires it.

## Color and UI

- Do not default to purple/indigo gradients, glow, glassmorphism, or pill clusters.
- Avoid the common AI looks: warm cream + terracotta serif, or dense broadsheet/newspaper layouts.
- If no brand exists, use a simple 2–3 color palette. No multi-layer decorative gradients.
- Dark mode is not the default unless requested.
- Color and motion must serve hierarchy or usability—not decoration.
- No card-heavy heroes, fake stats, badge spam, or emoji-as-polish.

## When unsure

- Ask, or ship the smaller version. Do not pad the project to look finished.
